    content: `RATIOS, RATES & PROPORTIONS

RATIOS:
A ratio compares two quantities. If the ratio of dogs to cats is 3:2 and there are 25 pets total:
Dogs = (3/5) × 25 = 15
Cats = (2/5) × 25 = 10

RATES:
Rate × Time = Distance (or Work)
Speed = Distance / Time

Example: Sarah took the subway 5 miles in 17 minutes.
Convert to hours: 17/60 hours
Speed = 5 / (17/60) = 5 × (60/17) ≈ 17.6 mph

UNIT CONVERSIONS:
Set up conversion fractions so units cancel.
Example: Convert 5,000 meters to feet (1 foot = 0.3048 m):
5000 m × (1 foot / 0.3048 m) ≈ 16,404 feet

PROPORTIONS:
If 3 teaspoons = 1 tablespoon and 16 tablespoons = 1 cup:
12 teaspoons × (1 tbsp/3 tsp) × (1 cup/16 tbsp) = 12/48 = 1/4 cup

DIRECT vs. INVERSE VARIATION:
- Direct: y = kx (as x increases, y increases)
- Inverse: y = k/x (as x increases, y decreases)

GRAVITATIONAL FORCE EXAMPLE (inverse square):
Fg = Gm₁m₂/r²
If r doubles, r² quadruples, so Fg becomes 1/4 of the original.

SAT TIP: When a rate problem involves two trips (going and coming back), the total speed is TOTAL DISTANCE / TOTAL TIME — NOT the average of the two speeds.`,
