    content: `DATA INTERPRETATION & GRAPHS

READING GRAPHS:
Always identify: title, axes labels, units, and scale before answering.

SCATTERPLOTS:
- Positive correlation: both variables increase together (upward trend)
- Negative correlation: one increases, the other decreases (downward trend)
- Line of best fit: minimizes the total distance to all data points
- Outliers: points far from the general trend

TWO-WAY TABLES:
Example: Male/Female × Cat/Dog owners
- "What fraction of males own cats?" → use the MALE ROW total as denominator
- "What fraction of cat owners are male?" → use the CAT COLUMN total as denominator
The word "of" tells you the denominator!

GRAPH TREND QUESTIONS:
"Between which years did enrollment stay steady?" → Look for a flat (horizontal) segment.

DENSITY PROBLEMS:
Density = Mass / Volume
If graphed as mass vs. volume, the SLOPE = density.
Weight of steel wall (300 m³ at 7,850 kg/m³) = 300 × 7,850 = 2,355,000 kg

READING FIGURE TRENDS:
For "what would X be in future year Y at the same rate?" → Find the rate of change per year and extend.

SAT TIP: On two-way tables, watch for "conditional probability" questions. They always give you additional information that restricts which group you're looking at.`,
