    content: `DESMOS CALCULATOR: TIPS & TRICKS

The Digital SAT has a built-in Desmos graphing calculator available for the ENTIRE Math section. Learning to use it well can save huge amounts of time — but you still need to understand the underlying math, since Desmos is a tool, not a replacement for knowing concepts. Practice at desmos.com/calculator before test day so the interface feels familiar.

1. LINEAR REGRESSION (find a line from data points)
If a problem gives you several (x, y) points that should form a line, create a table in Desmos (click the + icon, choose "table"), enter the points, then on a new line type:
y1 ~ mx1 + b
Desmos will solve for m (slope) and b (y-intercept) automatically using regression.

Example: Points (1, −2.5), (−2, −13), (7, 18.5) → typing the regression line gives m = 3.5, b = −6.

2. SLIDERS FOR UNKNOWN CONSTANTS
Type an equation with an unfamiliar letter (like "a") instead of a number, and Desmos offers to turn it into a slider. Drag the slider to test different values until the graph matches the condition described in the question (e.g., "no solution" means two lines become parallel).

Example: For 6x − 5y = 8 and y = ax + 3 to have NO solution, slide a until the lines are parallel. They become parallel at a = 1.2.

3. SOLVING SYSTEMS OF EQUATIONS GRAPHICALLY
Type both equations directly into Desmos. The point where the two lines cross IS the solution — Desmos will show you the exact (x, y) coordinates if you click the intersection point.

4. SOLVING ABSOLUTE VALUE EQUATIONS
Use the keypad's absolute value bars, graph the expression, and read off where the graph crosses your target value. For |x − 4| = 9, the graph crosses at x = −5 and x = 13.

5. VISUALIZING INEQUALITIES
Type inequalities directly (using < , >, ≤, ≥ from the keyboard icon) and Desmos shades the solution region. To check if a point satisfies a system of inequalities, see if it falls inside the overlapping shaded region.

6. PARABOLAS — INSTANT VERTEX & ROOTS
Graph any quadratic and Desmos automatically marks the vertex and x-intercepts (roots) with gray dots you can click for exact coordinates. No need to complete the square by hand.

7. GRAPHING CIRCLES
Type the circle equation (x − h)² + (y − k)² = r² directly — Desmos renders the circle so you can visually confirm center and radius, which is especially useful for checking your work.

8. MEAN & MEDIAN SHORTCUTS
Under the function list, Desmos has built-in mean( ) and median( ) functions. Type "mean(2,5,9,10,18,20,40,38)" and it instantly returns the value — much faster than sorting and adding by hand.

9. DECIMAL-TO-FRACTION SHORTCUT
After typing a decimal, click the fraction icon in the keypad to instantly convert it to a simplified fraction — useful for SAT answers that must be entered as fractions.

10. QUICK PERCENT CALCULATIONS
Type a percent calculation directly, like "20% of 700" using the % key, and Desmos computes it immediately — no need to convert to decimal form yourself.

SAT TIP: Desmos saves time on graphing-heavy problems, but you should still know the underlying concept (vertex formula, slope formula, etc.) so you can solve quickly even if you don't reach for the calculator, and so you can sanity-check whatever Desmos shows you.`,
